buildabook
==========

buildabook turns a plain-text outline into a typeset book. One outline file
drives the chapter numbering, the table of contents, and every
cross-reference, so the book is a build artifact of a single source of order.

buildabook is a build tool for long manuscripts, written in Ada. It reads
`outline.txt`, resolves `{{token}}` cross-references inside the chapter source
files, and emits a Typst `book.typ` plus a `number_map.csv` audit. It is the
shared build script of three book projects, extracted into one program.

Why it exists
-------------

A long manuscript has a property that a single prose pass does not: every
number and every cross-reference is a claim about the whole book, and those
claims go stale the moment the structure changes. An LLM is good at prose and
bad at that bookkeeping. It writes "see Chapter 14" and, ten chapters later,
inserts a chapter so that 14 becomes 15 without noticing the reference.

buildabook removes that failure mode by separating identity from rendering.
The writer, human or LLM, never writes a number. It writes a stable reference,
`{{c:id00042}}`, and the number is computed at build time from the row order of
`outline.txt`. The UUID (`id00042`) is a name, not a number: its digits are not
the chapter's number, and a chapter named `c:id00042` can render as "Chapter 1"
if it sits first in the outline. Inserting, deleting, or reordering a chapter
is one line in the outline; every reference in the prose follows on the next
build. A number is never baked into a title, so a renumber never rewrites
prose.

The same mechanism covers the other places an LLM invents instead of computes:

* Page numbers are never written by hand. `{{page:id90001}}` resolves through a
  native Typst lookup, so a reference stays correct across repagination.
* The table of contents is generated from the outline, never typed.
* A token's prefix is checked against the target's kind, so a wrong prefix or
  a typo'd UUID fails the build instead of shipping a wrong reference.
* `buildabook check` fails on duplicate UUIDs, unmatched files, and
  unresolvable tokens before any PDF is produced.

The division of labour is deliberate. The LLM writes prose and stubs in the
outline and chapter files; buildabook does the numbering, the references, and
the consistency checks, which are the parts a prose pass cannot hold in its
head.

What it does
------------

* **Numbering from order.** Chapters, parts, appendices, sections, and
  sub-entries are numbered from their row position in `outline.txt`, never
  from their UUID digits.
* **Directives.** `label`, `number`, `count`, `default`, and `none` change the
  rendering regime for the entries of a prefix that follow them. The regime is
  snapshotted onto each entry, so a cross-reference renders with the regime in
  force at the target's position.
* **Cross-references.** `{{c:id00042}}`, `{{c:id00042|n}}`, `{{c:id00042|s}}`,
  `{{cc:id00001,00002}}`, `{{toc}}`, and the `s:`/`k:` forms resolve at build
  time.
* **Page references.** `{{page:ID}}`, `{{c:id00042|page}}`, and `{{anchor:ID}}`
  compile to native Typst `locate()` lookups, one pass, always exact.
* **Number map.** `number_map.csv` lists every entry's UUID, kind, title,
  regime, computed number, and rendered designation, for auditing a regime
  change before compiling.
* **No giant tables.** Markdown tables longer than a page are split with the
  header row repeated.
* **Cover spine.** `buildabook cover` rewrites the spine width in `cover.typ`
  from the PDF page count, then compiles the cover.

The input language is specified in `LANGUAGE.md`.

Building
--------

buildabook is Ada 2022, built with gprbuild, and links the hbnf runtime
(`hbnf_config.gpr`, which pulls in mustache-ada transitively). The canonical
toolchain is the ada-on-alpine overlay, packaged as the `ada-toolchain:edge`
container image.

    ./configure
    make
    make check        # the aunit suite
    make install      # installs buildabook and its man page

`configure` checks for gprbuild, the GNAT compiler, and that the hbnf and
mustache-ada projects are gprbuild-visible (and that aunit is visible, for
`make check`). `build.sh` wraps the whole build in the container if you prefer
that to a host toolchain.

Usage
-----

    buildabook               typst -> pdf -> cover, building what it can
    buildabook typst         render rendered/book.typ + number_map.csv
    buildabook pdf           compile rendered/book.typ with Typst
    buildabook cover         update the cover spine, compile cover.typ
    buildabook check         validate outline, filemap, and tokens; emit nothing
    buildabook number-map    write number_map.csv only
    buildabook uuid [c|p|a|d|n|s|k]  print the next free UUID

A command-line flag overrides `book.conf`, which overrides the built-in
default. The default (`buildabook` with no subcommand) builds all three stages
in order, and a missing or failing stage never blocks the next: no `cover.typ`
just means the cover is skipped, never the book or the PDF.

How it works
------------

The pipeline is one source, one document, one pass:

    chapters/*.md + outline.txt + filemap.txt
          |
    buildabook typst   parse the outline, number it, resolve every {{token}},
          |            split oversized tables, and emit rendered/book.typ
    typst compile rendered/book.typ   ->   the PDF

The engine parses `outline.txt` with an hbnf-generated parser, applies the
directives in one positional pass, and snapshots the regime onto each entry.
It then builds a token map (`c:id00042 -> "Chapter 42"`, and so on) and rewrites
each chapter source, substituting tokens and planting page markers. The
resulting Typst document imports a template and calls `#chapter(...)` once per
entry.

Configuration
-------------

`book.conf` supplies per-book metadata and build paths, in obconf syntax
(bareword or `"quoted"` directives, `{ }` blocks, `#` comments):

    title "Telegraph to Turbo Jet"
    author "David Walther"
    slug   speedrun-telegraph-to-turbo-jet

    build {
        outline    outline.txt
        src        chapters
        out        rendered
        template   book.header.typ
        number-map number_map.csv
    }

    cover {
        source cover.typ
        output speedrun-cover.pdf
    }

Every field is optional and every `--`-flag overrides it. The `slug` derives
the PDF and cover output names; the `build` and `cover` blocks name the input
and output paths. A malformed file is reported as `file:line:col: message`.
It is parsed by hbnf's `HBNF_Config` crate, the same obconf grammar the outline
uses.

The template
------------

The engine ships a default Typst template (`template/book.typ`). A book may
override it. The coupling contract an override must honour is documented in
`LANGUAGE.md`: define `book-title`, `running-title`, `fm-pages`, `body-pages`,
`chapter(path, token, part, toc)`, `pgan(id)`, `pageof(id)`, and import
cmarker.

Limitations
-----------

* **Line-oriented.** A title or directive value runs to end of line, so it
  cannot contain a literal newline. There is no trailing-comment syntax.
* **Five-digit UUIDs.** Each entry is addressed by a five-digit UUID (or
  `idNNNNN.MINOR` for a sub-entry). That is a finite namespace, and the writer
  must look up a UUID rather than a number when writing a reference. The
  `number_map.csv` is the look-up aid.
* **Sections need their token for page refs.** A `{{page:...}}` reference to a
  section requires that section's heading to carry its token
  (`## {{ s:id02104 }} — Title`), because that token is what plants the page
  marker.
* **Sub-entries assume their parent.** A `k:idNNNNN.MINOR` entry binds to the
  chapter with UUID `idNNNNN`; the engine does not verify that chapter exists at
  parse time.
* **Monolithic output.** The book is one Typst document. Volume II or a
  split-book workflow is a Makefile-level concern, not an engine feature.
* **Toolchain weight.** Ada, gprbuild, hbnf, and Typst are more to install
  than a Python script. In exchange the numbering and reference machinery is
  checked at compile time and the output is reproducible.

Repository hooks
----------------

The three de-claude git hooks in `githooks/` strip and block commits that credit
or mention Claude or Anthropic. Enable them in a clone with:

    make install-hooks        # or: git config core.hooksPath githooks

